meta {
  name: 05 Start checkout
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/payments/{{paymentId}}/checkout
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.paymentId: eq {{paymentId}}
  res.body.checkoutUrl: isDefined
}

docs {
  Where the center goes to pay for a PENDING payment. Run
  `02 Create payment` first — it captures `{{paymentId}}`.

  ## The server must be running with a provider

  With nothing configured this answers 503 PAYMENT_PROVIDER_NOT_CONFIGURED.
  That is the default on purpose: forgetting a variable leaves payments
  unavailable, never open.

  For local testing, start the API with:

      PAYMENT_PROVIDER=fake
      FAKE_PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<any value of 32 characters or more>

  The fake refuses to switch on if NODE_ENV is `production` or the secret is
  shorter than 32 characters. NEVER set PAYMENT_PROVIDER=fake on DigitalOcean.

  With the fake, `checkoutUrl` points at `checkout.fake-payments.invalid`. The
  `.invalid` domain never resolves, so it can never be mistaken for a real
  payment page. To finish the payment, use `06 Simulate provider webhook`.

  ## What to check by hand

  1. SEND IT TWICE. Both return the SAME `checkoutUrl`. The second call is
     answered from the database and never asks the provider again — a second
     provider transaction for one payment could be paid as well.

  2. THE PROVIDER REFERENCE IS NOT IN THE RESPONSE. The client needs somewhere
     to go, not the provider's transaction id. With the fake it is
     `fake_{{paymentId}}`, stored in `payments.provider_reference`:

         SELECT provider_reference, checkout_url FROM payments WHERE id = '...';

  3. A PAID OR FAILED PAYMENT -> 409 PAYMENT_NOT_PENDING. Finish the payment
     with `06`, then send this again. A failed payment is finished too: the
     center creates a new payment instead.

  4. ANOTHER CENTER'S PAYMENT -> 404, never 403. A 403 would confirm the id
     exists.

  ## While BLOCKED

  This works. Creating a payment a lapsed center could not then go and pay
  would leave it exactly as locked out as before.
  `test/center-blocked-surface.spec.ts` pins it.

  ## Rate limit

  20 checkouts an hour per center, on a budget separate from creating
  payments, so clicking "pay" a few times never uses up the slots needed to
  create one.
}
